course: dictionary
lesson: 13945
title: Lexical: to do with words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lexical means to do with words and their meanings. It is about vocabulary, the words a language has. «Her lexical skills are strong: she knows lots of words and what they mean.» Here lexical tells us the skill is about words, not about numbers or drawing.
explain: Many words in English are lexical words. Nouns, verbs and adjectives are lexical words because they carry meaning. «The happy dog ran fast.» Happy, dog, ran and fast all carry meaning, so they are lexical words.
explain: Small words like «the», «of» and «to» are grammar words. They hold a sentence together, but they carry less meaning on their own. When you study vocabulary, the lexical words are the ones to learn first.
fact: Lexical means to do with words and their meanings.
fact: Lexical means to do with vocabulary, the words of a language.
fact: Lexical words are nouns, verbs and adjectives, such as «dog», «run» and «happy».
fact: Grammar words, such as «the» and «of», hold sentences together.
fact: Dictionaries and word studies are lexical work, because they look closely at words and what they mean.
exercise: What does lexical mean? | To do with running fast | To do with numbers and sums | *To do with words and their meanings | To do with the weather
exercise: Which of these is a grammar word? | *and | run | dog | happy
exercise: Ella says, «Her lexical skills are strong.» What is being praised? | Her running speed | *Her knowledge of words | Her height | Her drawing
test: Is «happy» a lexical word? Choose the best answer. | *Yes, it is an adjective that carries meaning | No, it is a grammar word | Only in capital letters | Only when it is a noun
test: Which sentence is about vocabulary? | The sun is hot. | *Learning new words builds your lexical knowledge. | We walked to school. | Two plus two is four.
exam: Which group is made of lexical words only? | the and of | *dog and run | at and in | to and a
